{"nodes": [{"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_revenue_centric_design___philosophy___process", "label": "Revenue-Centric Design \u2014 Philosophy & Process", "file_type": "document", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "document", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_the_9_principles_of_revenue_centric_design__rcd_", "label": "The 9 principles of Revenue Centric Design (RCD)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: The 9 principles of Revenue Centric Design (RCD)", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Intentional design serves the user AND the business at once \u2014 value and revenue, not one or the other. Richard's canonical framework, named Revenue Centric Design (RCD), built after Dieter Rams' 10 laws (form/function) and Amber Case's Calm Technology (attention/context) \u2014 \"neither taught me to think about revenue.\""}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_design_s_leverage_isn_t_constant___it_changes_with_the_product_stage", "label": "Design's leverage isn't constant \u2014 it changes with the product stage", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Design's leverage isn't constant \u2014 it changes with the product stage", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Design's payoff is near-zero at MVP and grows to decisive at scale; when a product is dying, design is the *last* place to look for the culprit. Knowing your stage tells you whether design moves the cash or is just vanity."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_design_owns_the_flow__not_the_final_coat_of_paint", "label": "Design owns the flow, not the final coat of paint", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Design owns the flow, not the final coat of paint", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "What decides whether a user converts or churns \u2014 information order, when you ask for the card, what appears at moments of doubt, when value is first felt \u2014 is set and coded long before a \"finished\" product reaches design."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_find_the_leaks_before_you_rebuild_the_bucket", "label": "Find the leaks before you rebuild the bucket", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Find the leaks before you rebuild the bucket", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Products rarely die from one dramatic error; they bleed out as micro-disappointments accumulate across the journey until the user quits without quite knowing why. Patch the leaks instead of redesigning from scratch \u2014 a fraction of the effort for most of the gain."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_refactor_to_solve_a_real_problem__not_to_repaint_the_wall", "label": "Refactor to solve a real problem, not to repaint the wall", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Refactor to solve a real problem, not to repaint the wall", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Designers loop forever (\"it's great \u2192 could be better \u2192 better \u2192 repeat\"), refactoring UI like code. True refactoring waits for user feedback and changes what fixes a problem; repainting because the old color got boring is vanity that burns a week on pixel-perfect nobody asked for."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_mine_the_tactical_layer___it_s_the_most_under_explored", "label": "Mine the tactical layer \u2014 it's the most under-explored", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Mine the tactical layer \u2014 it's the most under-explored", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Product design has three leverage drivers \u2014 Tactical \u2192 Organizational \u2192 Strategic. Strategic has the most asymmetric upside, but because everyone outsourced aesthetics to the same AI-generated UI kit, the tactical layer (aesthetics + function) became the most under-explored opportunity in the stack: lowest leverage in theory, highest return in practice, simply because nobody looks."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_leave_the_over_used_parts_alone__improve_around_them", "label": "Leave the over-used parts alone; improve around them", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Leave the over-used parts alone; improve around them", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Heavily-used parts of a working product form a \"cognitive map\" \u2014 users memorized where everything is and which gesture does what \u2014 that is part of the product even if you never designed it intentionally. Redesigning it aggressively makes them pay a re-learning cost and signals you think you know better than they do."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_don_t_hire_a_designer_to_make_software__pretty_", "label": "Don't hire a designer to make software \"pretty\"", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Don't hire a designer to make software \"pretty\"", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Aesthetics is subjective, doesn't scale, and won't save a product from high churn. The interface's job is to steer user behavior toward a KPI; aesthetics is sometimes a by-product of that. Hiring design for looks is technical founders' biggest financial mistake."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_treat_the_interface_as_data__not_opinion", "label": "Treat the interface as data, not opinion", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Treat the interface as data, not opinion", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One kind of founder, when churn rises, opens analytics \u2014 maps where users stalled, hesitated, which screen preceded cancellation \u2014 and treats interface as data. The other debates color palettes in product meetings. One is building a company, the other a portfolio."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_measure_changes__don_t_argue_from_opinion", "label": "Measure changes; don't argue from opinion", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Measure changes; don't argue from opinion", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "\"Change the color, swap the CTA, kill the pop-up\" \u2014 and nobody tests anything. Faith in gut beats faith in data science. Product design is experimentation and analysis, not guesswork: if you don't test, how will you improve, and if you don't improve, you don't grow."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_better_design_wins_even_when_the_tech_is_worse", "label": "Better design wins even when the tech is worse", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Better design wins even when the tech is worse", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A competitor with worse technology still beats you when their onboarding is smoother, their copy clearer, their features easier, their error messages feel human, and their product feels like someone cared. That sum is \"better design\" \u2014 and it's why they're winning and you're not."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design_make_the_dashboard_answer__what_do_i_do_now__", "label": "Make the dashboard answer \"what do I do now?\"", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: Make the dashboard answer \"what do I do now?\"", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A dashboard is your software's front door, not NASA mission control. Cram it with colorful charts, five-decimal counters, and endless tables and the user takes a cognitive-overload beating, feels dumb, and churns. A good dashboard answers one question \u2014 \"What do I do now to get more value?\" \u2014 and that drives LTV."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design__earlier_draft__the_10_design_principles", "label": "(Earlier draft) The 10 design principles", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "section: (Earlier draft) The 10 design principles", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An earlier morning draft of what later became the canonical RCD framework above \u2014 explicitly \"focused on influencing behavior and generating revenue.\""}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design__richardrx___2026_05_05", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-05", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-05-05", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2051672248348479691"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design__richardrx___2026_06_15", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-15", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-15", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2066476811177877962"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design__richardrx___2026_06_09", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-09", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/revenue-centric-design.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-06-09", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2064327349894553855"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_revenue_centric_design__richardrx___2026_06_04", "label": "@ri